::ENG2_U05_L04_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Sentence: "During the science demonstration, students had to _____ how the liquid changed color." Which word is most precise?{
~A. ignore#Incorrect. Ignore is the opposite of what the students need to do.
=B. observe#Correct. Observe means watch carefully to learn or understand.
~C. forget#Incorrect. Forget does not fit the task.
~D. decorate#Incorrect. Decorate is unrelated.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which word is the antonym of precise?{
~A. exact#Incorrect. Exact is a synonym of precise.
~B. accurate#Incorrect. Accurate is related to precise.
~C. specific#Incorrect. Specific is related to precise.
=D. vague#Correct. Vague means unclear or not exact, so it is an antonym of precise.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which pair contains synonyms?{
=A. analyze and examine#Correct. Both can mean to look at closely.
~B. vague and exact#Incorrect. These are opposites.
~C. careful and careless#Incorrect. These are opposites.
~D. ignore and observe#Incorrect. These are opposites in many contexts.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Dictionary entry: Analyze means to examine carefully by separating into parts. Which paraphrase is accurate? Sentence: "Students will analyze the paragraph's structure."{
~A. Students will ignore the paragraph's structure.#Incorrect. Analyze does not mean ignore.
~B. Students will memorize the paragraph without thinking.#Incorrect. Analyze requires examination.
=C. Students will examine the paragraph's structure carefully by looking at its parts.#Correct. This uses the dictionary meaning accurately.
~D. Students will decorate the paragraph's structure.#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Why is clear better than bright in "The speaker gave a _____ explanation"?{
=A. Clear describes an explanation that is easy to understand.#Correct. Bright can mean clear in some contexts, but clear is precise for an explanation.
~B. Bright is always a noun.#Incorrect. Bright can be an adjective, but it does not fit best here.
~C. Clear means colorful.#Incorrect. Clear means easy to understand in this context.
~D. Bright is an antonym of explanation.#Incorrect. Antonyms are opposites, not mismatched word uses.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] Which word-family clue helps explain structure?{
~A. re means again#Incorrect. Re is not part of structure.
~B. pre means before#Incorrect. Pre is not part of structure.
~C. un means not#Incorrect. Un is not part of structure.
=D. struct means build#Correct. Structure connects to something built or arranged.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which sentence uses inspect most precisely?{
~A. Students inspect a song by singing loudly.#Incorrect. Inspect means examine closely, not sing.
=B. The mechanic will inspect the engine for problems.#Correct. Inspect means examine closely for details or problems.
~C. Students inspect lunch by eating quickly.#Incorrect. This does not fit the meaning.
~D. The students inspect the sky by forgetting it.#Incorrect. This is illogical.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which word best completes the sentence? "The directions were _____, so everyone knew exactly what to do."{
~A. vague#Incorrect. Vague directions would not help everyone know exactly what to do.
~B. confusing#Incorrect. Confusing does not fit.
=C. precise#Correct. Precise means exact and clear.
~D. unclear#Incorrect. Unclear is the opposite of the needed meaning.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Which paraphrase keeps the meaning of "The directions were precise"?{
~A. The directions were confusing.#Incorrect. This is the opposite meaning.
~B. The directions were missing.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
~C. The directions were decorative.#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
=D. The directions were exact and clear.#Correct. This keeps the meaning of precise.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which reference material is best for checking a word's pronunciation and definition?{
=A. dictionary#Correct. A dictionary gives definitions and often pronunciation.
~B. title page#Incorrect. A title page does not give full definitions and pronunciation.
~C. thesaurus#Incorrect. A thesaurus mainly gives related words.
~D. table of contents#Incorrect. This organizes a book.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which reference material is best for finding possible synonyms?{
~A. glossary only#Incorrect. A glossary defines terms in a text.
~B. index#Incorrect. An index locates topics.
=C. thesaurus#Correct. A thesaurus lists synonyms and related words.
~D. title page#Incorrect. A title page does not list synonyms.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Why should a thesaurus be used carefully?{
~A. It always gives wrong words.#Incorrect. A thesaurus can be useful.
=B. Related words may have different precise meanings and tones.#Correct. Writers must check context before choosing a synonym.
~C. It replaces the need for context.#Incorrect. Context is still necessary.
~D. It only gives antonyms.#Incorrect. It often gives synonyms and related words.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which word best completes the sentence? "The principal made an _____ announcement about the exam schedule."{
=A. important#Correct. Important fits because the announcement matters to students.
~B. invisible#Incorrect. Invisible does not fit the context.
~C. delicious#Incorrect. Delicious describes taste, not an announcement.
~D. noisy#Incorrect. Noisy does not fit the meaning.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which word relationship connects precise and exact?{
~A. antonyms#Incorrect. They are not opposites.
~B. homophones#Incorrect. They do not sound the same.
~C. unrelated words#Incorrect. They are related in meaning.
=D. synonyms#Correct. They have similar meanings.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which word relationship connects vague and clear?{
~A. synonyms#Incorrect. They do not mean the same thing.
~B. word family#Incorrect. They are not from the same base.
=C. antonyms#Correct. Vague and clear are opposites in meaning.
~D. identical meanings#Incorrect. They differ strongly.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Dictionary entry: Inspect means to examine closely. Which paraphrase is accurate for "The coach inspected the equipment"?{
~A. The coach ignored the equipment.#Incorrect. This is the opposite of inspected.
=B. The coach examined the equipment closely.#Correct. This keeps the meaning of inspected.
~C. The coach forgot the equipment.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
~D. The coach painted the equipment.#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which word has the strongest intensity?{
~A. annoyed#Incorrect. Annoyed is milder.
~B. bothered#Incorrect. Bothered is milder.
=C. furious#Correct. Furious is stronger than annoyed or upset.
~D. calm#Incorrect. Calm is not angry.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which word best fits this context? "The scientist needed to _____ the sample under a microscope."{
=A. inspect#Correct. Inspect means examine closely.
~B. glance#Incorrect. Glance means look quickly.
~C. avoid#Incorrect. Avoid means stay away from.
~D. forget#Incorrect. Forget does not fit.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.V.02] Which word is connected to the struct/build word family?{
~A. consult#Incorrect. This is not part of the struct/build word family.
=B. structure#Correct. Structure is connected to something built or arranged.
~C. contrast#Incorrect. This is not the struct/build family.
~D. constant#Incorrect. This is not part of the family.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which sentence uses major most precisely?{
~A. The ant was major on the sidewalk.#Incorrect. Major does not fit this context.
~B. The pencil was major yellow.#Incorrect. Major does not describe color this way.
~C. She major walked to class.#Incorrect. Major is not used this way.
=D. The schedule change was major because it affected every exam.#Correct. Major means important or large in effect.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Which paraphrase is accurate? Sentence: "The speaker gave a clear explanation of the policy."{
=A. The speaker explained the policy in a way that was easy to understand.#Correct. This keeps the meaning of clear explanation.
~B. The speaker hid the policy from listeners.#Incorrect. This contradicts the sentence.
~C. The speaker gave no explanation.#Incorrect. This contradicts the sentence.
~D. The speaker explained only the color of the policy.#Incorrect. This misuses clear.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which word best completes the sentence? "The witness gave a _____ description, including exact times and locations."{
~A. vague#Incorrect. Exact times and locations are not vague.
~B. careless#Incorrect. Careless does not fit exact details.
=C. precise#Correct. Precise means exact and detailed.
~D. blurry#Incorrect. Blurry suggests unclear.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which explanation best describes shade of meaning?{
~A. a punctuation mark after a word#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
~B. the color of printed words#Incorrect. This is not the vocabulary meaning.
~C. the number of letters in a word#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
=D. a small difference in meaning or intensity between related words#Correct. Shades of meaning are subtle differences.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which choice is the least precise for academic writing?{
~A. analyze#Incorrect. Analyze is precise.
=B. do stuff#Correct. This phrase is vague and informal.
~C. observe#Incorrect. Observe is precise in the right context.
~D. inspect#Incorrect. Inspect is precise.
}



::ENG2_U05_L04_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.V.03] Which strategy is strongest for choosing a precise word?{
=A. Read the context, check reference materials, compare related words, and choose the word that best fits the exact meaning.#Correct. This process uses context and reference tools responsibly.
~B. Pick the longest synonym in the thesaurus.#Incorrect. Longest does not mean most precise.
~C. Choose any synonym without checking context.#Incorrect. Synonyms are not always interchangeable.
~D. Ignore word relationships and guess.#Incorrect. Word relationships help determine meaning.
}
